The '''Kingdom of Iskhal''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Boqortooyadii Iskhal'') was a monarchy centred on the city of [[Iskhal]], located on the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast approximately 50 km southwest of the mouth of the [[Buran River]] of [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. It emerged from the independent city-state of Iskhal around 300 AD when the [[Urtog Dynasty]] consolidated hereditary rule, and existed until 403 AD, when the marriage of '''[[Bugad the Great]]''' to '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''' united Iskhal with the neighbouring [[Republic of Mazar]] to form the [[Kingdom of Burawa]].

== Origins ==

=== Founding myth ===
According to tradition, the cities of [[Iskhal]] and [[Mazar]] were founded by twin sons of an ancient king who ruled the coastal settlements near the [[Buran River]]. The son who "touched the sky" was given the name '''Iskhal''' and received the inland territory; the son who "touched the sea" was given the name '''Mazar''' and received the coastal peninsula. The '''Iskhal River''', a minor tributary of the Buran, marked the boundary between the two domains. The historicity of this account is not established, but the tradition was widely accepted in both cities and was cited in the diplomatic negotiations surrounding the 403 AD merger.

=== Early history ===
The coastal settlements near the lower [[Buran River]] were among the sites brought under [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]] tributary rule during the '''[[Dagaalka Buran]]''' (~700–650 BCE). Following the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia#Geedi collapse|Geedi collapse]] (~500 BCE) and the arrival of [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] traders (~600 BCE), the area grew into one of the principal centres for the export of highland iron. At some point during the post-Geedi period, the coastal community divided along the lines described in the founding myth: the peninsula settlement retained the name Mazar and developed a republican form of government, while the inland settlement on the eastern bank of the Iskhal River became the city-state of Iskhal.

Throughout the period of the [[Ancient Burawi city-states]], Iskhal functioned as one of several independent coastal polities, governed by an assembly of leading families. Its economy was based on trade with the [[Buran River]] corridor to the northeast — iron, copper, and timber from the [[Kushish]] highlands passed through the region en route to the coast — and on the agricultural hinterland.

== Geography ==

Iskhal occupied the inland territory on the eastern bank of the '''Iskhal River''', approximately 50 km southwest of the [[Buran River]] mouth. The city controlled the landward approaches to the coast and the roads connecting the region to the Buran valley and the interior. This position gave Iskhal control over overland trade routes and the ability to field armies from a larger territorial base than the peninsula-bound [[Republic of Mazar]].

Over time, Iskhal expanded across the Iskhal River to the western bank, gradually surrounding the [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] peninsula on its landward sides. By the 4th century AD, Mazar was effectively enclosed by Iskhal territory on three sides, with only its seaward face open to the [[Mesogean Sea]].

== Transition to kingdom ==

Around 300–350 AD, the city-state's governing assembly was supplanted by the '''[[Urtog Dynasty]]''', a military family that established hereditary rule over Iskhal and its surrounding territory. The circumstances of this transition are not fully attested in surviving sources. Dalmar-era inscriptions at [[Badalga-Jir]] refer to "kings of the river mouth" in the 4th century, and the Urtog Dynasty appears to have consolidated power through control of the Iskhal garrison and the city's land-based military forces.

The kingdom that emerged was a compact but militarily capable state. The Urtog kings maintained a professional garrison, collected customs on goods passing through the region toward the coast, and exercised authority over the farming communities of the immediate hinterland.

== Relations with Mazar ==

The relationship between Iskhal and the [[Republic of Mazar]] was defined by geographic proximity and economic complementarity. Iskhal controlled the land and the river; Mazar controlled the harbour and the Mesogean fleet. Neither could function without the other: Iskhal's trade goods needed Mazar's ships to reach the Mesogean market, and Mazar's merchants needed Iskhal's overland routes to access the highland supply chains.

This interdependence produced periods of both cooperation and rivalry. The two cities competed for preferential terms with [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] and [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] trading firms, and disputes over customs revenue on goods passing between the interior and the harbour were recurrent. Iskhal's territorial expansion across the Iskhal River, which progressively surrounded the Mazar peninsula, increased pressure on the republic and contributed to the political conditions that led to the 403 AD merger.

== Bugad the Great and the founding of Burawa ==
{{main|Kingdom of Burawa}}

In 403 AD, '''[[Bugad the Great]]''', prince of the [[Urtog Dynasty]], married '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''', a member of the ruling mercantile family that governed the [[Republic of Mazar]]. The marriage united the two polities under a single crown, creating the '''[[Kingdom of Burawa]]''' under the Urtog Dynasty. Iskhal's military tradition and royal legitimacy were combined with Mazar's commercial wealth and naval fleet.

The [[Urtog Dynasty]] continued as the ruling house of the Kingdom of Burawa after the merger. The city of Iskhal remained an important administrative and military centre within the new kingdom, though the commercial capital shifted increasingly to [[Mazar]] and its harbour.

